Z||000002||Will||02-06-2001||04:28 PM||||@#$%^& Internet! I am getting tired of this piece of *&^% eating my posts!<P>I thought what he had to say about natural frequency was worthwhile, but he there were also a few head scratchers in that piece.<P>1) He says that a high roll center is an advantage for a solid axle suspension because of reduced body roll in corners. While this is strictly correct, it is incomplete... I also seem to remember that Herb Adams in "Chassis Engineering" states that a solid axle has a low roll center, but don't quote me on that.<P>2) He doesn't like live axles because they are "very subject to wheel hop". I guess that's why live axle 'Stangs and F-Bodies launch hard very easily while IRS 'Vettes and Supras are more difficult to get off the line.<P>3) Another dire warning about "Jacking Effects" with precisely zero explanation of what weight jacking is. He doesn't even elucidate the relationship between high roll center and weight jacking.<P>4) He doesn't give Colin Chapman any credit for the chapman strut, but knows plenty about where the MacPherson strut came from.<P>5) He spelled precision wrong (should I let that slide?).<P>6) "For increased traction, use wider rims and/or wider tires." Umm... Duh.<P>In short, don't believe everything you read on the internet. Exercise your right to take things with a grain of salt.<P>The Grinch<p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 02-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||FIEROCIOUS||02-08-2001||01:03 AM||||Thanks for the info Will. <BR>Hope you enjoyed it none the less.?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Will||02-08-2001||02:38 PM||||As I said, the part about natural frequency and ride comfort was interesting. The rest is nothing special, available in correct form in any performance suspension book. I'm not saying it's a bad article, I'm just saying it has good points and bad points.<P>How much do you already know about suspension? If you really want to learn, you are going to need to get a book. The whole enchilada is just not out in the internet in sufficient detail.<P>Go to <A HREF="http://www.motorbooks.com" TARGET=_blank>www.motorbooks.com</A>  and do a search for "suspension". I recommend Chassis Engineering  by Herb Adams as a good starter book. It covers many topics in suffcicient detail to gain a good understanding, while not getting too in depth for a novice.<p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 02-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||FIEROCIOUS||02-08-2001||05:27 PM||||Will, already have it that and quite a few others, I'd have to say there is better info than what is contained in Cassis Engineering (like you said not very in depth) but good to have around in case you forget what an A-arm is! LOL <P>Will, one thing all the books I have forget to give any information on, is the proper pivot point for steering linkage on a stut-type suspention for zero bumpsteer. I would speculate inline with the suspention points like an A-arm system but does the strut off set change this? Let me know if you have any information or if you can direct me to a book or site wich would supply that info! Thanks for any help you can give.||||reg||1
Z||000006||FIEROCIOUS||02-08-2001||05:30 PM||||Oh, and also if you know of any software with strut design available? All the software I've seen and use, do not include strut supention geometry. Thanks again!||||reg||1
Z||000007||Will||02-09-2001||10:07 AM||||I bought CAR (computer aided racing) from motorbooks.com. It supposedly does struts. It is a complete waste of money. It turns out huge steaming, reeking heaps of $h!t under the guise of useful information. They'll give any monkey a CS degree these days... (you're not a computer programmer, are you?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> )<P>For zero bump steer on a strut system, the inner tie rod end needs to be in the plane formed by the inner pivots of the A-arm and the pivot at the strut top. I <I>think</I> that the outer tie rod end should be in a plane with the outer A-arm pivot and the strut top pivot. Thinking about a bump steer kit?<P>I was supposed to get "Tires, Suspension, and Handling" by John Dixon for Christmas, but I'm still waiting.||||reg||1
Z||000008||FIEROCIOUS||02-09-2001||12:46 PM||||Will, sorry not a computer programmer, how'd ya guess? I agree with yo on the CS degree my buddy is in the process of getting one now, sad thing is after two years I still know more about computers than he! Wich isn't much I might add!<BR>well idealy if I were GM and designed a rear suspention I would think it would be easier to make a single pivot point were the balljoint currently is and call it done. But if I were to go back and re-engineer cheaply designed crap I would shorten the tierod and put in plane as you suggested (my guess as well) <BR>Thanks for the warning on the book! All the books I have are just pieces of a whole one book will go into detail more on a specific area than the other and vice versa. I have most of what is contained in each book memorized by now any how and I just go back if I need a refresher from time to time.<BR>I did find a company to order brackets and heimjoints or any other race/suspention parts you may need @ <A HREF="http://www.hoerr-racing.com" TARGET=_blank>www.hoerr-racing.com</A>  they have a free catalog and recomend ordering it.||||reg||1
Z||000009||ThatRickGuy||02-09-2001||01:15 PM||||Just throwing out my views,<P>on the initial comments:<BR>2) He doesn't like live axles because they are "very subject to wheel hop". I guess that's why live axle 'Stangs and F-Bodies launch hard very easily while IRS 'Vettes and Supras are more difficult to get off the line.<P>That was the main complaint of the 2000 mustangs, was the indipendent rear end, every review I read on the new mustang said wheel hop is a problem.<P>and for #5. yes. In internet debating it is usually considered a low blow to use a grammer/spelling mistake as an argueing point. unless the person is: "ah god speel'r" in which case, redicule is required.<P>and just as a side note, I am a computer programmer :P hehehe, no CS tho, I'm in the military, working on my MCSD.||||reg||1
Z||000010||gixxer||02-09-2001||08:46 PM||||Fier-o-chuz, and others, try Amazon dot com and pick a couple books up:<BR>"Fundamentals of Vehicle Dynamics" by Gillespie.<BR>And "Race Car Vehicle Dynamics" by Milliken.<BR>Millikin's  book is much more interesting.<BR>Once you read these books, you will not have to ask anybody questions about suspensions ever again.<BR>ALL the info you will ever need will be there.<BR>Look up "Performancetrends.com" or whatever, they have a lower price program with A-arm and MacPherson strut capabilities. <BR>They have a 3D big buck program with full bump steer, etc. capabilities, but only for double A-arms. <BR>All the knowledge is out there, but it's in BOOKS, definitely not the internet. <BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||FIEROCIOUS||02-11-2001||01:39 PM||||gickser,?... Thanks for the info. I don't have the book by Milliken yet I'll give it a go! Thanks! ||||reg||1
Z||000012||FIEROCIOUS||02-11-2001||01:45 PM||||ThatRickGuy, The one design flaw IRS has is the fact that very little anti-squat can be used Mustangs and Camaros have quite a bit of anti-squat designed into their suspentions. Given the same amount of anti-squat, and depending on were the shocks are placed, the IFS would have less axle hop than the stait axle. ||||reg||1
Z||000013||ray b||02-11-2001||08:34 PM||||traction bars on live axel stop hop but hurt cornering and ride not a option on irs like ours . pro street vetts use live axel +bars<BR>irs and dragrace dont mix at hi HP.||||reg||1
